        "name": "NeoTech",
        "email": "andreas@roughedge.se",
        "date": "2026-11-07T23:36:06Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(motion): read a short turned bar's axis off its facing flats\n\nThe axis fit refused any part whose normal moment is isotropic (both\nsmaller eigenvalues past ISO = 0.5 of the largest) as \"every axis\nfits\". A turned bar at 0.5 < L/D < 2 balances its end caps against its\nwalls and sits in that window, so an r10 x 40 bar with one modelled\nradial hole was refused by every lathe cross and axial drill.\n\nThe fit now also offers the part's flat poles as candidate axes\n(axis_poles.rs): sign-folded normals one cluster of facets shares with\nat least 5 % of the area, at most three. They are judged by the same\nconcurrency and coarseness gates. When the plain line about a pole is\ndragged by an off-centre feature, it is re-centred by least trimmed\nsquares first. An isotropic part that no pole earns is still refused\nby name: a sphere has no flat, and a cube's faces fail the coarseness\ngate. Eigen candidates are unchanged and win ties, so an even revolve\nkeeps its bits.\n\nPinned by a_short_punched_bar_reads_as_turned_about_its_axis\n(drilling_cross_spindle) and the axis_poles unit tests (L/D 2 and\nL/D 1.5 bars fit; a cube and a sphere are refused by name).\n\nFixes #152\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
