  {
    "sha": "1a42edf77bd4582a1cb392bd63b9e70ecfe281e6",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVVonwNoAKDFhNDJlZGY3N2JkNDU4MmExY2IzOTJiZDYzYjllNzBlY2ZlMjgxZTY",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "NeoTech",
        "email": "andreas@roughedge.se",
        "date": "2026-11-08T00:43:41Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "NeoTech",
        "email": "andreas@roughedge.se",
        "date": "2026-11-08T00:43:41Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(motion): a pencil's rings keep one latitude; flats ride with a ring\n\nSecond re-review of PR #223:\n- B1: a single-band Z shaft crossed at the origin by a rod tilted\n  5..45 degrees read the rod's axis, or a line between the two, as\n  trusted. Seen from a tilted axis the shaft's wall is a squashed ring:\n  its bearings stay spread, so the per-pencil bearing moments read it at\n  only (1 - cos ang)/2.\n- B2 (regression): a square plate with a centred bore was refused: its\n  sides sit in the bore's pencil, and their four bearings broke the cap.\n\nEach pencil is now judged by latitude (n . u), the angle at which a\nrevolve's ring meets its axis. The facets are sorted into latitude\nclasses, and each narrow class must hold a ring (bearing skip within\ntwice the sagitta budget). Flats at the ring's latitude ride with it:\na D-flat, a keyway floor, the plate's sides round its bore. A 90-degree\ncross folds onto two bearings at many latitudes and holds none. And the\nlatitude must not run with the bearing: the share of its variance\nexplained by the first two bearing harmonics (trigonometric regression)\ncounts as off the axis once the swing passes 2e-3. Both are capped\ntogether at 10 % of the inlier area. The bearing-moment test is gone.\n\nPins:\n- a_skewed_cross_never_reads_the_rod_or_a_line_between (5/15/30/45 deg,\n  rod h60 and h100, 1 and 5 bands): fails on e883ae6.\n- a_square_plate_with_a_centred_bore_reads_its_axis (r20 t10, r40 t10,\n  r45 t30): fails on e883ae6.\n\nFixes #208\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
